<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kahramanmaraş</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maraş, Marqas, Germanicia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kahramanmaraş is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was Marqas, capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Gurgum, and later, as Germanicia and then Maraş, it was a frontier town between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Islamic world. It received the epithet Kahraman, 'heroic', for its resistance to French occupation in 1920, and it was badly hit by the earthquakes of February 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras.jpg" alt="Kahramanmaraş" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrat · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kahramanmara%C5%9F_city_center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kahramanmaraş is the administrative centre of Kahramanmaraş Province in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The city lies on a plain at the foot of Mount Ahır, 568 metres above sea level, and the Ceyhan River, which rises in the mountains around the Elbistan Plain, is its most important hydrological feature. Historically it was known as Marash, Maraş in Turkish, and Germanikeia in Greek. After 1973 Maraş was officially renamed Kahramanmaraş, adding kahraman, the Turkish word for heroic, to commemorate the Battle of Marash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, from the late 11th century BCE to about 711 BCE, the city was the capital of the Syro-Hittite state of Gurgum, called the Kurkumaean city by its Luwian inhabitants and Marqas by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>. In 711 BCE Gurgum was annexed as an Assyrian province and named Marqas after its capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the city Germanicia Caesarea. In Byzantine times Germanikeia was the seat of an eparch, one of whom took part in the First Council of Nicea. It was lost to the Muslims in the 7th century, and under al-Mansur the whole Christian population of the Germanikeia valley was deported and resettled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p><p>After the fall of the Armenian kingdoms in the 11th century, the city became a stronghold for exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and the capital of the short-lived principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a>. His successor, the Armenian general Tatoul, hosted the exhausted army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> for four days. According to Matthew of Edessa, an earthquake destroyed the city on 29 November 1114 and killed 40,000 people.</p><p>It passed between the Danishmends, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, among them Tancred, who retook it in 1107. From 1337 to 1515 it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of Dulkadir as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Mamluk Empire before being annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In 1525–6 it had 1,557 adult males, a total population of 7,500, all Muslims, and a substantial number of non-Muslims migrated there later, mainly in the 19th century.</p><p>In 1915 about 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> from Marash were deported. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> the French army occupied Maraş, and in February 1920 Turkish nationalist forces regained control, followed by a massacre of the Armenian population. The official French report said the victims did not exceed 5,000. In December 1978 the Maraş Massacre of leftist Alevis, incited by the Grey Wolves, left more than 100 dead and contributed to the declaration of martial law and the 1980 coup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kahramanmaraş had 571,266 inhabitants in 2022 and is populated mostly by Turkish and Kurdish people, with a small Armenian population. The earthquakes of 6 February 2023 had their epicentre in Pazarcık and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a> in the province and destroyed much of the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kahramanmaraş” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around them gathered the Saadlu, Karamanlu, Alpaut, Agacheri, Dukharlu, Jagirlu and Hajilu tribes, from Nakhchivan and Ganja to <b>Maraş</b>, Erzurum and Ardabil; their own traditions say they came to Anatolia from Central Asia with the Mongol conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake with an estimated magnitude of 7.4 to 7.7 struck the area of Marash, now <b>Kahramanmaraş</b>, and also hit Antioch, Mamistra and Edessa in the crusader lands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ala al-Dawla Bozkurt, a long-reigning ruler, built in <b>Marash</b> and resisted the rising Safavids.</blockquote><a href="/state/dulkadirids">Dulkadirids</a></li><li><blockquote>The administration encouraged Armenian resettlement in Adana, <b>Marash</b> and Aintab, which alarmed Turkish Muslims.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cilicia">French Cilicia</a></li><li><blockquote>Gurgum arose after the Hittite collapse in the valley of the Aksu river around <b>Marqas</b>, modern Kahramanmaraş, in south-eastern Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/state/gurgum">Gurgum</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat at Manzikert in 1071 and the civil wars that followed, he gathered soldiers and Armenian refugees and made himself master of <b>Marash</b>, Melitene and other towns in the Taurus and Cilicia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-philaretos-brachamios">Principality of Philaretos Brachamios</a></li></ul></section>
